  "problem": "Let $x\\in R.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x<-1.$ \n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\n- $\\sqrt{5}\\in R$\n- $\\sqrt{2}\\in Z$\n- ${x}^{5}>1$",
  "worked_solution": "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{\u23de}{P}}\u21d2\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x\\in R,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
